## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 4 has been about staying alive: 4.0 named the systems to defend, 4.1 the drill to run when a number moves, 4.2 the right member to seat in each defense slot. **4.3 turns the drawer over.** These are not protective ancillaries and not anabolics — they're the *third drawer*, the compounds whose only job is the **look and the output**: fat-burners, thyroid-for-cutting, fullness, pumps, vascularity, stage tools. The article's own framing is blunt about where this sits: *"This is the shiniest of the shiny objects... the margin on the margin — overwhelmingly cut-phase and contest tools layered on a physique that's already built. Most of the year, most people don't need any of it."*

Three ideas the poster must make a reader *feel* instantly, all of which a generic chat will miss if it just draws fat-burner pills:

**1. Read the INDIRECT effect — that's the one you're actually running it for.** The article's central interpretive device, stated and italicised: *"Reading a compound by its indirect effects is how you actually predict what it'll do to your physique and performance."* Every entry is structured **direct mechanism → direct effect → the prominent indirect effects** — and the indirect column is the real reason it's in the drawer. Clenbuterol's *direct* job is thermogenesis; you run it for the *indirect* muscle-sparing, oxygenation, and vascularity. Cardarine *directly* switches fuel to fat; you run it for the *indirect* endurance and glycogen sparing. MK-677 *directly* raises GH/IGF-1; you run it for the *indirect* fullness, sleep, and joint cushion. So the per-compound grammar of this poster is a small **two-stage transducer**: mechanism goes in, but the **big lit plate is the indirect output** — the eye is trained to read the downstream consequence, not the receptor.

**2. The tier is set by your body fat, not your impatience.** The single most important discipline in the drawer: *"You match the accelerator intensity to how lean you already are — the deployment pipeline escalating to meet the body-fat tier, never the reverse."* Light (soft / opening a mini-cut) gets the gentle base; medium (a genuine mid-cut) adds the mitochondrial layer; heavy (already lean, chasing a final peak) earns the full stack. And critically: *"Stepping straight to the heavy stack while soft just burns through your CNS and your insulin sensitivity for nothing — and the moment a monitored marker turns the wrong way, the tier steps back down."* This is a **governor on an accelerator**, not a free throttle: the body-fat read is the interlock that decides how far up you're cleared to go, with a return pawl that drops you a tier when monitoring flags.

**3. There is one line that has actually killed people — and it has no dose on purpose.** *"Contest diuretics... the single most dangerous thing in this entire series — it has killed competitors outright via potassium/electrolyte crashes and cardiac arrhythmia. There are no reference doses here, on purpose."* This is categorically different from the gentle BP-driven indapamide *restraint* of 4.2. It is the article's own designated red line, which makes it the perfect — and only — red object for the poster.

Under those three sit two more structural truths the instrument should carry: a **cost axis** (the stimulant fat-burners *flog the CNS* at real cardiovascular cost; the newer mitochondrial/glucose layer works *underneath* the CNS at far lower cost — same fat-loss goal, very different height on the cost scale), and **read-by-goal-not-by-drug** (the article's second table reorganises the whole drawer by *desired look/output* — lean-out, fullness, endurance, dryness/vascularity, insulin sensitivity — with several compounds feeding one goal and a few dual-use compounds feeding two).

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** *This drawer doesn't build muscle — it reveals and sharpens the muscle the anabolics already built (the margin on the margin). You read each tool by its indirect effect (the real reason you run it), you only climb the accelerator as far as your body fat unlocks (the tier is set by leanness, not impatience, and steps back down the instant monitoring flags), and you never, ever touch the one redline past the top — contest diuretics — which is drawn with no dose because that line has killed people.*

